  "the_drop_scales_with_density_pitch_and_depth": "worst case 200 um burial, 2000 um pitch, 50 W/mm^2: 99.4 K between junction and extractor. Pitch matters more than depth -- at 50 W/mm^2 and 200 um burial the drop runs 10.1 K at 100 um pitch and 99.4 K at 2000 um.",
  "BUT_THE_PHI_ERROR_IS_SMALL_AND_INVERTED": "the overstatement in phi never exceeds 8.2% across a 100x density range, and it is NON-MONOTONIC: 6.1% at 0.6 W/mm^2, peaking at 8.2% near 2 W/mm^2, then falling to 6.8% at 10 and 2.8% at 50. It is NOT simply worst at low density -- an earlier reading of this table said so and was wrong. The fall at the top is phi saturating: at 872 K against 972 K it moves only 0.742 -> 0.763, so a 99 K drop costs under 3%. The rise at the bottom is the opposite limit, where phi is small enough that even a 1 K drop is a few percent of it. The peak sits where neither effect dominates.",
  "verdict": "reading phi off junction temperatures is defensible to within 8.2% everywhere in this sweep, which is smaller than most other uncertainties in the framing. The prerequisite is discharged: Tests 1, 3 and 4 do not need correcting.",
  "caveat": "the 10 and 50 W/mm^2 rows put silicon at 200-970 C, far outside its viability, and this probe runs without leakage feedback. They are a CONDUCTION result and are the right regime in which to ask the phi question, not a claim that such a die would work."
 },
