  "MEASURED": "the gradient is roughly LINEAR in removal and desperately shallow: about 0.0348 K per watt removed at best. 20 W removed from a 60.7 W die -- a third of its total power, concentrated on 30% of its area -- buys 1.39 K.",
  "extrapolated_to_the_book_s_target": "holding the 150 K split Section 10.8 asks for would need roughly 4307 W of removal on a die that dissipates 60.7 W -- about 71x the die's entire power budget.",
  "AND_IT_CONFIRMS_THE_CLOSED_FORM_MODEL": {
   "analytic": "thermal_zones.py: G = k_Si * boundary_length, boundary = 124.6 mm for this zone definition, k = 135 W/m.K -> G = 16.77 W/K, so 150 K needs 2516 W",
   "solver": "3D-ICE: 4307 W",
   "agreement": "1.71x",
   "why_it_matters": "two completely independent methods -- a closed-form lateral-conduction argument and a full 3D-ICE solve -- agree within a factor of 1.7 on a quantity spanning three orders of magnitude. The analytic model is the optimistic one, as expected: it counts only the direct boundary path."
  },
  "verdict": "THE MONOLITHIC THREE-ZONE TEMPLATE IS NOT BUILDABLE. This is the negative result the test was run to find, and it is better to have it before pitching than after. It does not refute Section 10.8 -- it relocates it: the cold zone has to be a physically separate die with a deliberate thermal break in the package, which makes this a packaging and disaggregation programme before it is an architecture one.",
  "TWO_INDEPENDENT_FAILURE_MODES_AND_BOTH_MUST_BE_FIXED": {
   "1_tile_pitch_too_coarse_to_ADDRESS_the_zones": {
    "measured": {
     "100um_pitch": "0.0348 K/W",
     "500um_pitch": "0.0309 K/W",
     "2000um_pitch": "0.0002 K/W"
    },
    "what_happens": "at 2000 um the gradient essentially vanishes -- 0.01 K for 40 W removed, 170x worse than at 100 um. A tile that straddles the zone boundary cools both zones equally, so the array simply cannot address them separately. This is the coarse-tile penalty the project already documents, appearing here as TOTAL loss of zone separability rather than as reduced efficiency.",
    "implication": "zone architectures need FINE pitch. The device's own first-generation 4-16 tiles, which the checklist records as 2.5-5.0 mm on the 34-core die, is coarser than the 2000 um case that fails completely."
   },
   "2_lateral_conduction_shorts_the_zones_even_at_fine_pitch": {
    "measured": "0.0348 K/W at the best pitch -- still ~70x short of what 150 K needs",
    "what_happens": "silicon conducts the gradient away as fast as the array removes it",
    "implication": "fixing pitch alone does not help; the conduction path has to be broken too"
   },
   "the_point": "these are INDEPENDENT. A fine-pitch array on a monolithic die still fails on conduction; a disaggregated die with a coarse array still fails on addressing. Both have to be solved, which raises the bar on the template rather than offering two chances to clear it."
  },
  "A_REFINEMENT_TO_THE_ANALYTIC_MODEL": {
   "observed": "a 50 um die outperforms a 200 um die: 0.0348 vs 0.0215 K/W at 100 um pitch",
   "does_this_contradict_thickness_cancellation": "no, and the distinction is worth keeping straight",
   "why": "thermal_zones.py's cancellation is about the LATERAL boundary conductance, G = k*P, where thinning shrinks cross-section and healing length together. Die thickness ALSO sets the VERTICAL path from the array down to the active layer, and that path is not self-cancelling -- a thinner die couples the cooling in better, so more of the removal reaches the silicon. Net: thinning does not help the lateral short, but it does help the vertical delivery, worth ~1.6x here. Both effects are real and they are different mechanisms."
  }
 },
 "WHAT_SURVIVES": "the premise is intact and measured: 34.6% of die power is static and 89% of that is in L3 alone, so the leakage IS concentrated and separable in the power sense. What fails is holding the gradient in one piece of silicon."
}